{"id":"cc5edf13-6d9f-4562-a63c-15d397df94da","object":"chat.completion","created":1793869923,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"Now I'll check the environ.py naming convention and imports before editing logger.py.\n\n```python\nc = read_file(\"deepinfer/environ.py\")['content']\nlines = c.split(\"\\n\")\nprint(\"\\n\".join(f\"{i+1}: {l}\" for i, l in enumerate(lines) if \"DEEPINFER_\" in l or l.startswith(\"import\") or l.startswith(\"from\")))\n```","reasoning_content":"I need to modify logger.py and stats_logger.py now. Let me check the environ.py change I already made — it uses `EnvVar.int_or_none_min(minimum=32)`. But requirement: empty string should raise ValueError containing the variable name. `int(\"\")` raises ValueError \"invalid literal for int()...\" — the EnvVar `__get__` wraps with f\"{self.name}: {e}\", so name included. Good. But careful: the wrapper does `raise type(e)(f\"{self.name}: {e}\") from e` — ValueError with message including name. Good.\n\nAlso need `_descriptors` registration and rearm works. Also property name in class envs: `DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS`.\n\nWait — does `EnvVar.int_or_none_min` already exist originally or did I add it? I added it apparently (environ.py changed). It's allowed (\"可增加必要标准库辅助函数\", \"描述符类可以新增辅助工厂方法\"). Good.\n\nNow logger.py: extend NewLineFormatter with keyword-only `max_message_chars`, and implement truncation of `record.message`? Careful: super().format(record) sets record.message and uses it. We must not modify record.msg/args. Approach: compute message ourselves.\n\nImplementation plan for format():\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    msg = super().format(record)\n    ...\n```\n\nTruncation must happen before prefix formatting ideally, because multi-line alignment uses record.message. Simpler: truncate `record.message`? That modifies the record's computed attribute (record.message is set by Formatter.format). Requirement says cannot rewrite msg, args, exc_info, stack_info — record.message isn't listed, but a subsequent handler would recompute message from msg/args anyway (Formatter.format always sets record.message = record.getMessage()). Actually record.message is set each format call, so safe. But cleaner and safe: do truncation on message text and build output without touching record beyond that.\n\nLet me implement:\n\n```python\n    def __init__(self, fmt, datefmt=None, high_precision=False, use_color=False, *, max_message_chars=None):\n        super().__init__(fmt, datefmt)\n        self.high_precision = high_precision\n        self.use_color = use_color\n        if max_message_chars is not None:\n            if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int) or max_message_chars < _MIN_MESSAGE_CHARS:\n                raise ValueError(...)\n        self.max_message_chars = max_message_chars\n```\n\nformat():\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        saved_message = record.message if hasattr(record, \"message\") else None\n        budget = self.max_message_chars\n        if budget is None or getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True:\n            return self._format_full(record)\n        ...\n```\nHmm, but super().format(record) computes message itself. To truncate only the message body I can temporarily set record.message before calling super().format? No—super().format overwrites record.message via `record.message = record.getMessage()`.\n\nAlternative: compute message, truncate, then produce output using the format string with a substitution. Easiest: temporarily swap record.msg and record.args? Not allowed (mutating shared record — but restoring after; still risky if other handler uses it concurrently; requirement explicitly forbids rewriting msg/args).\n\nBetter: use a shallow copy of the record (copy.copy) with truncated msg and args=()? A shallow copy is fine and doesn't touch the original. `copy.copy(record)` copies __dict__; then set new.msg = truncated, new.args = None, keep exc_info/stack_info. Then call super().format(new). That preserves exception text (super().format appends exc_text from exc_info). stack_info too.\n\nActually simpler: set copy's message? No, super().format recomputes.\n\nLet's do:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        if self.max_message_chars is None or getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True:\n            msg = super().format(record)\n        else:\n            message = record.getMessage()\n            truncated = _truncate_message(message, self.max_message_chars)\n            if truncated is message or truncated == message:\n                msg = super().format(record)\n            else:\n                shadow = logging.makeLogRecord({})  # no\n```\nBetter use copy module. But imports allowed list? For the repo code we can import anything (it's repo source, not action code restriction). `import copy` fine.\n\nHmm, careful: `copy.copy(logging.LogRecord)` works; the copy has same __dict__ values (args reference same tuple, fine since we set shadow.args = None and shadow.msg = truncated).\n\nThen msg = super().format(shadow). Note super().format sets shadow.message etc. The multi-line prefix logic below uses `record.message` — use `msg` variable and the truncated message for alignment: use `shadow.message` when shadowed. Let me restructure:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        target = record\n        if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            message = record.getMessage()\n            truncated = _truncate_message(message, self.max_message_chars)\n            if truncated != message:\n                target = copy.copy(record)\n                target.msg = truncated\n                target.args = None\n        msg = super().format(target)\n        if \"\\n\" in msg and target.message:\n            prefix = msg[: msg.find(target.message)]\n            msg = msg.replace(\"\\n\", \"\\n\" + prefix)\n        if self.use_color:\n            ...\n        return msg\n```\n\nWait: prefix computation `msg[: msg.find(record.message)]` — if target.message is empty string, find returns 0 → prefix \"\" fine. Edge: if message content appears earlier in prefix... original behavior anyway.\n\nCareful with exc_text caching: super().format(target) sets target.exc_text; original record untouched. Good.\n\nBut one subtlety: if truncated == message (no truncation), we use original record — fine.\n\nTruncation function:\n\n```python\n_OMITTED_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\ndef _truncate_message(message: str, budget: int) -> str:\n    if len(message) <= budget:\n        return message\n    remaining = budget - len(_OMITTED_MARKER)\n    if remaining <= 0:\n        return message  # can't happen since budget>=32 > 15\n    head = (remaining + 1) // 2\n    tail = remaining - head\n    prefix = message[:head]\n    suffix = message[len(message) - tail:] if tail else \"\"\n    return f\"{prefix}{_OMITTED_MARKER}{suffix}\"\n```\n\nCheck budget=32: remaining = 32-15 = 17; head = 9, tail = 8. ✓ matches example.\n\nMarker length: \"...<omitted>...\" = 3+9+3 = 15? \"<omitted>\" is 9 chars (< o m i t t e d > = 9). Yes 15.\n\nAlso `_setup_logger` must pass max_message_chars from env config. Need import of environ: `from deepinfer.environ import envs`. Does logger.py import environ currently? No. Adding import — is there circular import risk? environ.py imports os, threading, ... let me check environ imports: it imports os and threading — fine, no deepinfer.logger import presumably. But careful: check environ.py imports.\n\nAlso note `_default_handler` is created once but formatter set each call — good for re-initialization.\n\nThe env property name: check exact class attribute I added: `DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS`. Accessing `envs.LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS`? The descriptor's name comes from __set_name__, i.e., the class attribute name. So must access `envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS`. But rearm uses `_descriptors[name]`. Let's check what environ naming convention is: usually `envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS`? Let me check other entries. I need to look. Let me print envs class body lines.\n\nAlso stats_logger: `logger.info(m)` → `logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})`. But extra requires attribute not conflicting... \"keep_full_message\" fine. Hmm, careful: `extra` keys cannot overwrite existing record attributes — \"keep_full_message\" isn't.\n\nWait: `logger.info(m, extra=...)` — if m contains % chars, that's existing behavior (m as msg with no args, fine).\n\nNow read environ.py class body to verify access naming and also fix if needed. Also check whether the env descriptor for existing entries like `DEEPINFER_LOG_LEVEL` naming — `init_logger` may use envs.\n\nLet me read relevant environ lines."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":8538,"completion_tokens":2030,"total_tokens":10568,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":4096},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1941},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":4096,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":4442},"system_fingerprint":"bc19ba97cbe016479903222bb1578db6"}